From e58406cf046975971707906c12e3091893894a7a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 08:03:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] packetreader: handle EOF in initPartialReader

initPartialReader only handled read() returning a negative value, so on
EOF (n == 0) it added 0 to the index and returned prcBuffer.  The pager
then never noticed that the buffer had died.

On systems where poll() reports POLLHUP for a closed socket this was
masked, because handleError cleaned up afterwards.  With the select()
based poll emulation (used on macOS) there is no POLLHUP, so the fd is
reported readable again on the next iteration, read() returns 0 for the
second time, and the pager dies with:

  dynstream.nim(24, 5) `not s.isend`  [AssertionDefect]

hiding the actual error of the buffer process.  Treat n == 0 as prcEOF,
like the recvMsg branch below already does.

Assisted-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context)
---
 src/io/packetreader.nim | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/io/packetreader.nim b/src/io/packetreader.nim
index a121b80f..93318d82 100644
--- a/src/io/packetreader.nim
+++ b/src/io/packetreader.nim
@@ -75,6 +75,7 @@ proc initPartialReader*(stream: PosixStream; pr: var PartialPacketReader):
       let e = errno
       if e == EAGAIN or e == EWOULDBLOCK or e == EINTR:
         return prcBuffer
+    if n <= 0:
       return prcEOF
     pr.idx += n
     if pr.idx < pr.lens.len:
@@ -92,6 +93,7 @@ proc initPartialReader*(stream: PosixStream; pr: var PartialPacketReader):
       let e = errno
       if e == EAGAIN or e == EWOULDBLOCK or e == EINTR:
         return prcBuffer
+    if n <= 0:
       return prcEOF
     pr.idx += n
     if pr.idx - pr.lens.len < pr.r.buffer.len:
